Check for Missingness
Let’s examine the number and percentage of missing values for each variable.

sum_na &lt;- function(column) {
  sum(is.na(column))
}

na_counts &lt;- water |&gt;
  summarize(across(everything(), sum_na))

na_counts

# A tibble: 1 × 10
     ph Hardness Solids Chloramines Sulfate Conductivity Organic_carbon
  &lt;int&gt;    &lt;int&gt;  &lt;int&gt;       &lt;int&gt;   &lt;int&gt;        &lt;int&gt;          &lt;int&gt;
1   491        0      0           0     781            0              0
# ℹ 3 more variables: Trihalomethanes &lt;int&gt;, Turbidity &lt;int&gt;, Potability &lt;int&gt;


We can see ph, Sulfate, and Trihalomethanes contain missing values.

Recipe
Our EDA revealed missing values in ph, sulfate, and trihalomethan. Instead of dropping these rows, we will impute the missing values with the mean of each column.

water_rec &lt;- recipe(potability ~., data = water_train) |&gt;
  step_impute_mean(ph, sulfate, trihalomethanes) |&gt;
  step_normalize(all_numeric_predictors())

Final Fit and Evaluation
We will finalize the random forest workflow with its optimal hyperparameters, fit it to the entire training set, and evaluate it one last time on the untouched test set.

# Select best parameters
best_tree_params &lt;- tree_res |&gt;
  select_best(metric = "mn_log_loss")

best_rf_params &lt;- rf_res |&gt;
  select_best(metric = "mn_log_loss")

# Finalize workflow
final_tree_wkf &lt;- tree_wkf |&gt;
  finalize_workflow(best_tree_params)

final_rf_wkf &lt;- rf_wkf |&gt;
  finalize_workflow(best_rf_params)

# Final fit 
tree_test_fit &lt;- final_tree_wkf |&gt;
  last_fit(water_split, metrics = metric_set(accuracy, mn_log_loss))

rf_test_fit &lt;- final_rf_wkf |&gt;
  last_fit(water_split, metrics = metric_set(accuracy, mn_log_loss))

# View test set metrics
tree_test_metrics &lt;- tree_test_fit |&gt;
  collect_metrics() |&gt;
  mutate(model = "Classification Tree")

rf_test_metrics &lt;- rf_test_fit |&gt;
  collect_metrics() |&gt;
  mutate(model = "Random Forest")

test_comparison &lt;- bind_rows(tree_test_metrics, rf_test_metrics) |&gt;
  select(model, .metric, .estimate) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = .metric, values_from = .estimate) 

test_comparison

# A tibble: 2 × 3
  model               accuracy mn_log_loss
  &lt;chr&gt;                  &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Classification Tree    0.621       0.663
2 Random Forest          0.671       0.617


Random Forest achieved lower mn_log_loss and higher accuracy on the test set than the Classification Tree.
